WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.870
Let's go next to, uh, let's go to Mike from
Georgia at the seven Oh six area code.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:06.870
Mike from Georgia.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:07.910
What's going on?

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:08.910
[inaudible]

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.009
Hey David, how are you doing?

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:12.009
Good, thanks.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:13.009
Okay.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:15.799
So, uh, I had a question.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:23.260
So, um, we hear a lot about, um, you know,
in the debate that they asked, uh, right-wing

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.750
framed questions.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.800
But don't we need a right-wing frame questions
if we're going to be taking on, you know,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.989
the uh, the Republican establishment or really
just the establishment as a whole.

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:34.989
Yeah.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.750
So, so you're asking a very important question.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:44.170
So here's the thing, some during, during democratic
debates, the point is to best figure out who

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:48.090
of the democratic candidates, really it's
for two things.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.719
Whose policies do you most agree with?

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:54.860
And we are also making judgments about electability.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.760
We are imagining the candidates on the stage
debating Donald Trump.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.859
So it seems like what you're pointing out
is at a certain point, the democratic nominee

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:08.850
is going to have to face Trump and is going
to have to face Republican arguments.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:15.549
Therefore, isn't it logical for the moderators
to be making some of those Republican arguments

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:16.740
in questioning the Democrats?

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.380
Is that sort of what you're saying?

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.840
Yeah, yeah.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.200
That's basically what I'm saying.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.920
Here's the problem with it.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.590
When the moderator is framing up a question
for all of the people on the stage that's

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:35.979
not about asking a particular candidate how
something about their policy will work.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:42.399
When you frame the entire question in a way
that is a Republican slant, you're in a sense

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:48.469
discrediting the fact that this is even being
discussed in particular terms and that's why

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.039
it's so problematic.

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:54.289
If you have different Democrats up on the
stage and someone wants a public option for

00:01:54.289 --> 00:02:00.619
health care, someone wants Medicare for all,
some someone wants a phased in Medicare, you

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.210
know you have all these, all these different
things.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:11.009
You want that to be debated in a way that
is sort of fair and neutral and let the candidates

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:15.040
frame their positions and if they want to
bring up what it is that Donald Trump wants

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.209
to do, fine.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:23.260
When the monitor moderators a priori frame,
the entire question where the logical answer,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.510
the right position is one.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.599
That is what Republicans are advocating for
or more to the right.

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:33.790
Not only is it not the point of the democratic
debates, it also is helping the more conservative

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.380
candidates up on the stage.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.971
And so we just don't want it.

00:02:36.971 --> 00:02:39.480
That's not the role the moderators were supposed
to have.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.480
Okay.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.480
Yeah.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.349
Yeah, that, that definitely makes sense.

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:48.549
Um, I always just kind of found a hypocritical
that they're supposed to come off as a neutral

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:54.860
force and yet they seem to go in more towards
a, uh, a Republican direction or really setting

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:00.080
up all these trap questions that you can't
really, you can't really wiggle your way out

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.569
of unless you attack the framing of the question.

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:07.930
That's another good point, which is that it
took to properly handle that you're putting

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:12.049
the candidates in a position where they either
have to go after the moderators for the framing,

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:13.049
which they should do.

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:16.989
And I'm glad when they do it, but that takes
time away from actually figuring out who onstage

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:18.239
has the best policy.

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:20.400
And so it's also a distraction in that way.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.650
Oh yeah.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.829
And, and that's kinda like my, my problem
with debate other than the fact that I don't

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:30.060
know how much they'll, they'll change the
course of the election.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.040
Cause I mean, I feel like most people kinda
have an idea of who they want to vote for.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.040
Yeah.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.409
At this point in the game, this late in the
game, the debates will do very little to change

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:47.079
public support other than if someone blunders
terribly and they will then be hurt.

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:51.379
But it is very difficult at this point in
the game to really gain very much by doing

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:54.760
well in the debates because people are mostly
familiar with the candidates and with their

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:55.760
positions.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:56.760
Yeah.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.190
All right, well you pretty much answered my
questions, so thank you very much David.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.190
All right, thanks Mike.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.019
Great to hear from you.

